   "subject": "Re: Observation model estimating g0 of 1, appearing to cause convergence issues when fitting models",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div><div dir=\"auto\">Hi Rory,</div></div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">Just so that I understand, what would the biology be that would create sigma~Bk. I’ve only ever used Bk on g0 as I envision it acting on the height of the detection function not necessarily the spatial extent (sigma). </div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">I would personally be ok using the g0~Bk and sigma~1. Unless there is a really good biological reason that sigma also needs Bk. Which there very well may be. </div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">Also, are al your spatial habitat covariates standardized? I’ve had most convergence issues when not standardized. But that’s not always the solution to convergence issues either. </div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">Cheers,</div><div dir=\"auto\">Clayton </div><div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
